<div class="almanac">
  <div class="almanac__main">
    <p class="lead">Welcome, reader, to a quiet almanac of history and general knowledge — a place to settle in by lamplight and learn something worth knowing. Here you will find long, unhurried reads on the events that shaped America and the world, portraits of lives worth remembering, plain explanations of how the everyday things came to be, and a note for each day of the year.</p>
    <figure class="figure figure--hero">
  <img src="assets/img/home-hero.webp" alt="A stack of old leather-bound books beside a lamp." width="1024" height="683">

</figure>
    <h2 id="sections">The almanac's pages</h2>
    <ul class="cardgrid"><li class="card"><h3><a href="american-history.html">American History</a></h3><p>Long, unhurried reads on the transcontinental railroad, Route 66, the space race, and the Erie Canal.</p></li><li class="card"><h3><a href="world-history.html">World History</a></h3><p>The printing press, the Silk Road, the Renaissance, and the Magna Carta — the turns that shaped the wider world.</p></li><li class="card"><h3><a href="biographies.html">Biographies</a></h3><p>Lives worth remembering, from Benjamin Franklin and Clara Barton to Marie Curie and Frederick Douglass.</p></li><li class="card"><h3><a href="everyday-explainers.html">Everyday Explainers</a></h3><p>How things came to be: time zones, the postal system, leap years, the seasons, and paper money.</p></li></ul>

    <hr class="fleuron" aria-hidden="true">

    <h2 id="welcome">A word from the keeper</h2>
    <p>There is an old and gentle pleasure in reading history for its own sake — not to pass a test or win an argument, but simply to understand how we came to be where we are. This almanac is made for that pleasure. We write long and plainly, in the belief that a good story well told is the finest way to learn, and that curiosity is a lamp that never dims with age.</p>
    <p>Everything here is free, and everything is written in our own words and checked with care. We cover history evenhandedly, present the lives of public figures with their virtues and their flaws, and explain the workings of the everyday world as clearly as we can. Pull up a chair, turn up the lamp, and read whatever catches your eye. There is no hurry in an almanac.</p>

    <h2 id="whats-inside">What you'll find on each shelf</h2>
    <p>The almanac is arranged into a few well-stocked shelves. The <a href="american-history.html">American History</a> shelf holds long, narrative reads on the great events that shaped the United States — the railroads and roads, the canal and the rockets — told in full and told honestly. The <a href="world-history.html">World History</a> shelf ranges wider, to the turning points that remade the whole world, from the printing press to the Industrial Revolution.</p>
    <p>The <a href="biographies.html">Biographies</a> shelf brings you close to the men and women behind the events, with honest portraits of their achievements and their humanity alike. The <a href="everyday-explainers.html">Everyday Explainers</a> turn from the grand to the familiar, revealing the hidden history and logic of ordinary things — the clock, the calendar, the mail, the money. And the <a href="on-this-day.html">On This Day</a> page keeps the old almanac custom of a note for each date of the year. Wander among them as you please.</p>

    <h2 id="why-read-history">The quiet pleasure of reading history</h2>
    <p>Why read history at all, when the past is done and gone? Because it is the richest story there is, and because we are all characters in its latest chapter. To read history is to understand how the world we live in came to be — why the map looks as it does, why we keep the customs we keep, why the great questions of our day have the shape they have. It lends perspective, that rarest and most valuable of gifts, and it is wonderful company besides.</p>
    <p>History read for pleasure, rather than for examination, is a different and gentler thing than many remember from school. There are no dates to cram, no tests to pass — only the unfolding of human stories, as absorbing as any novel and all the more remarkable for being true. We write with that pleasure in mind, telling the great events as stories, with their people and their drama intact. An evening spent in the past is never an evening wasted, and it leaves the mind richer and the world a little more wonderful than it found them.</p>

    <h2 id="who-for">For every curious reader</h2>
    <p>This almanac asks nothing of its readers but curiosity. You need no prior knowledge, no special education, only the wish to understand a little more of the world than you did yesterday. We explain as we go, define our terms, and never assume. Whether you are a lifelong student of history or someone who simply likes a good true story of an evening, there is a chair by the lamp for you here.</p>
    <p>We are especially mindful of older readers, for whom these pages are above all made. The type is large and grows larger at a tap, the lines are generous, and every article can be printed for comfortable reading away from the screen. History belongs to everyone, and we have tried to make this a welcoming and unhurried place to enjoy it.</p>

    <h2 id="no-rush">There is no hurry in an almanac</h2>
    <p>We live in a hurried age, and much of what we read is made to be skimmed and forgotten. This almanac is made for the opposite — for the long, slow, satisfying read, enjoyed at whatever pace pleases you, with nothing counting down and nothing demanding your attention elsewhere. An article here may take you twenty unhurried minutes; so much the better. The pleasure is in the lingering.</p>
    <p>So treat these pages as you would a good book on a quiet evening. Read one article tonight and another next week; return to a favorite; follow your curiosity down whatever path it opens. Nothing here expires, nothing must be finished by a deadline, and no one is keeping score. An almanac is a companion for the long haul, meant to be dipped into again and again across the seasons and the years. We hope it will be exactly that for you.</p>

    <h2 id="how-made">How the almanac is made</h2>
    <p>The Lamplit Almanac is an independent journal of history and general knowledge. It sells nothing and carries no advertising. Our articles are drafted, checked against reliable references, and reviewed before publishing, and each carries the date it was last reviewed. Where history is genuinely uncertain, we say so; where we quote, we quote only works long in the public domain. The type is large and grows larger at a tap, so the pages are a comfort to read of an evening. Begin wherever you please — the <a href="american-history.html">American History</a> shelf is a fine first stop, and the <a href="on-this-day.html">On This Day</a> page always has something for today.</p>
